PURPOSE
H128 permits:
temporary version forks
when:
regional adaptations diverge.

H129 asks:

WHAT HAPPENS
WHEN:
MANY REGIONS
KEEP CREATING
LOCAL DESCENDANTS?

A family can become:
a branching version tree
with:
growing memory,
shims,
and:
reconciliation cost.

Question:

WHEN SHOULD
A FORK:

RECONCILE,

REMAIN DORMANT,

or:

BECOME
A NEW TRANSLATOR FAMILY?

BOUNDARY
This is synthetic.

It does not:
- freeze the clustering threshold;
- prove latent family count is recoverable in general;
- establish production branch garbage collection;
- solve malicious branch creation;
- modify or execute STAB-18-R1.

EVIDENCE
SYNTHETIC
MEASURED_SANDBOX

REGIONAL ECOLOGY
12 local regions.

12 recurrent operating epochs.

Underlying interface semantics come from:
3 or 4 latent translator families.

Mean true family count:
3.297.

Regions can:

stay in:
their current semantic regime,

return to:
an earlier regime,

or:

switch to:
another family.

Thus:
old interface structure
has:
real recurrence value.

LOCAL BRANCH BIRTH
Each region
creates:
a local descendant
near:
its current semantic target.

Without compaction:

12 regions
x
12 epochs

can create:
144 branch objects.

COMPARATORS

RETAIN EVERY BRANCH
Preserve:
every exact regional version.

Use:
the latest exact local branch.

FORCE GLOBAL MERGE
At each epoch,
replace:
all local branches

with:
one global mean translator.

FLAT EXACT BRANCH CACHE
Retain:
up to:
18 exact branch versions.

Use:
nearest compatible cached branch
when:
available.

Evict:
old exact branches
when:
the cap is exceeded.

SPECIATION ECOLOGY
New local branch
is compared with:
existing translator-family models.

If:
sufficiently compatible,

it joins:
an existing family
and:
updates:
the family center.

If:
persistently outside:
all family fit envelopes,

it creates:
a new family.

Old families remain:
dormant
for:
a bounded recurrence window
before:
retirement.

