GENERAL PRINCIPLE
A VERSION FORK
SHOULD NOT
REMAIN:
AMBIGUOUS FOREVER.

Eventually:

MERGE
if:
semantics compose.

RETIRE
if:
one branch loses value.

or:

SPECIATE
if:
both remain:
persistently useful
and:
incompatible.

DECISION
AR-H129 is POSITIVE
for:
MEMORY-PRICED VERSION-FORK SPECIATION

WITH:
RETAIN-ALL
AND
FORCE-MERGE LIMITS.

Supported synthetically:

- retaining every local branch causes version-state growth;
- forcing one global version destroys heterogeneous regional semantics;
- a bounded exact cache preserves much recurrence value;
- branch speciation compresses many descendants into a small family ecology;
- learned family count closely tracks the latent family count in the tested toy;
- dormant family retention supports recurring old regimes;
- persistent incompatibility can justify stable family identity rather than failed repeated merging.

Not demonstrated:

- hierarchical family trees;
- family merge after long convergence;
- overlapping context membership;
- adversarial branch spam;
- cross-family translator inheritance;
- online memory-price adaptation.

